---
/**
 * foodcraft / CtaBandBL — "Kalinaw Coffee" CLOSING CTA BAND
 * (design element data-component="CTA-BAND").
 *
 * ROLE. The last ask on the page and the only place the design puts copy and a
 * photograph side by side with no gutter between them: a two-column espresso
 * band, the darkest ground in the template, carrying an amber eyebrow, the
 * largest Fraunces line after the h1, one sentence, and exactly two ways to act
 * — the owner's own CTA in amber and the owner's own phone number in the
 * outlined secondary — with a wide café photograph filling the other half edge
 * to edge.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="CTA-BAND" in
 * "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 *
 *   section     background #1C130C                       → --band-deep
 *               (NOT #241A12: this band is a shade deeper than the How band,
 *                which is what separates the closing ask from the mid-page one.
 *                --band-deep is that exact value, so this file states a token,
 *                not a hex, and the wrapper's `band-dark` still supplies every
 *                text and button flip that goes with an espresso ground.)
 *   container   max-width 1240px, centred, NO padding of its own
 *               (NOT .wrap — see THE CONTAINER below)
 *   grid        minmax(0,1.05fr) minmax(0,.95fr); align-items:stretch
 *   copy cell   padding 96px 44px 96px 32px;
 *               display:flex; flex-direction:column; justify-content:center
 *   eyebrow     10px / .42em caps / #E8A33C / 600         → .kick on .band-dark
 *   h2          Fraunces 400, clamp(34px,4.4vw,62px), lh 1.05,
 *               margin .22em 0 .32em, #FBF6EC, and — like GALLERY's — NO
 *               max-width                                → .h2 on .band-dark
 *   sub         17px / 1.75 / #CDBFAD, margin 0 0 30px, max-width 40ch
 *                                                        → --bone-soft-2
 *   actions     flex, gap 12px, wrap, align-items center
 *   primary     btnAmber — #E8A33C fill, #241A12 label, 12px/.15em caps,
 *               16px 32px, 100px radius                  → .btn.btn--band-accent
 *   secondary   1px rgba(251,246,236,.4), #FBF6EC label, same type,
 *               16px 30px, 100px radius                  → .band-dark .btn--line
 *   photo cell  position:relative; min-height 440px; background #2A1E14
 *                                                        → .plate + --band-well
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE (R1). Every colour the design names
 * already has a wrapper token holding the same value: #1C130C is --band-deep,
 * #2A1E14 is --band-well, #E8A33C is --band-accent, #FBF6EC is --bone, #CDBFAD is
 * --bone-soft-2, rgba(251,246,236,…) is rgba(var(--bone-rgb),…), 100px is
 * --r-pill. btnAmber is character-for-character the wrapper's .btn--band-accent and
 * the outlined action is character-for-character `.band-dark .btn--line` apart
 * from its 30px side padding, so this file adds exactly ONE button declaration.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (each one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 *    so every data-field below is a hook a real sidebar input owns) ──────────
 *   ctaBand.tag        — the amber eyebrow (.kick)
 *   ctaBand.headline   — the h2, bound with set:html so an owner's own <em>
 *                        takes the template's signature italic anywhere inside
 *                        THEIR sentence (on a band that italic is AMBER, not
 *                        rust — the wrapper owns that rule, see R3 below)
 *   ctaBand.sub        — the one sentence under it
 *   ctaBand.cta.text + data-href-field ctaBand.cta.href — the amber action
 *   location.phone     — the outlined action (data-field ONLY, see THE PHONE)
 *   ctaBand.image      — data-image-field, the right half's photograph
 * Nothing else. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook that no sidebar
 * input owns and that focuses nothing, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, never stored and never editable ────────────────────────────────
 *   · the CTA's href, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBL actually
 *     mounts, and its target/rel pair — one conditional per attribute, so
 *     neither is ever emitted twice
 *   · the tel: href, built from the phone's OWN digits
 *   · whether the band is two columns or one — see THE EMPTY HALF
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT (R2) ─────────────────────────────────────
 * NOT ONE VISIBLE WORD. The design's demo strings — "See you in the morning",
 * "Save yourself the queue.", "Message us your order and a pickup time…" and
 * the slot's "Drop a wide café photo" — are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 * template. A real business gets a closing band that says only what that
 * business said, so every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no amber eyebrow over
 * nothing, no headline above an empty column, no action row with no actions in
 * it, and no tinted rectangle standing in for a photograph nobody uploaded.
 *
 * ── NOT RENDERED, deliberately ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   · No id on the <section>. This page's anchors are menu / about / gallery /
 *     location / faq / main; the design gives CTA-BAND none of them and no nav
 *     link points at it, so inventing one would add a target nothing aims for.
 *     A derived '#book' resolves to '#main' through ANCHOR_ALIAS below.
 *   · No aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote; the h2 already titles the region.
 *   · No target="_blank" on the amber action. The design hard-codes one because
 *     its demo href is an external messaging URL, but here the same button may
 *     resolve to '#location', and opening an in-page anchor in a new tab is a
 *     bug. It is emitted for http(s) ONLY — same rule as HeaderBL.
 *   · The design's `margin-top:96px`. In that file every section is
 *     padding-top-only with a bare bottom, so the band owns the gap above it.
 *     PageBL instead declares a symmetric shared `section` rhythm, and the
 *     section that precedes this one — FaqBL — ships with that shared padding
 *     intact (`.ask` overrides nothing vertical), so its own bottom padding
 *     already IS that 96px. Adding the design's margin on top would open a
 *     ~192px hole above the band. (HowBL makes the OPPOSITE call for its own
 *     band, and correctly: GalleryBL above it zeroes its bottom padding to keep
 *     the design's bare-bottom mosaic, so there the margin is the only thing
 *     holding the two apart and HowBL carries it. FaqBL zeroes nothing.)
 *
 * ── THE CONTAINER ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The grid is NOT wrapped in .wrap, which is the one place this section departs
 * from the family's habit. .wrap is min(--maxw, 92vw) — the family's stand-in
 * for the "1240px box with 32px of padding" that every OTHER section in this
 * design has. This band's container is the one that carries NO padding: the
 * design moved that 32px inside the copy cell precisely so the photograph could
 * run to the container's edge. Wrapping it would apply the page gutter twice —
 * once as 4vw of espresso down both edges of the band, again as the copy cell's
 * 32px — which stops the picture 4vw short of the edge on every viewport under
 * ~1348px and pushes the closing copy 32px right of every other section's text.
 * So the container states the design's own two values (--maxw, centred).
 *   · The wrapper's shared `section` padding, which is zeroed here. The design
 *     puts ALL of this band's inset inside the copy cell (96px block) precisely
 *     so the photograph can reach the band's top and bottom edges; keeping the
 *     shared padding would float the picture in a 96px espresso frame and lose
 *     the flush half that is the whole point of the layout.
 *   · .reveal on the PHOTOGRAPH. The copy stack reveals like the rest of the
 *     family, but the photo is a full-height ground: translating it 14px would
 *     slide a sliver of --band-deep out from under it on every scroll-in.
 *   · A star row, a rating, a count, or any other number. Nothing in this band
 *     is a claim, so there is nothing here to fabricate.
 *
 * ── SECTION GATE (R5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The band renders when it has BODY content: an action (the CTA label or a
 * phone number), the sentence, or the photograph. It deliberately does NOT
 * count the eyebrow or the headline — lib/derive-content-defaults.ts fills
 * ctaBand.headline with "Ready when you are." on EVERY site, so a gate that
 * counted it would be a no-op that ships a headline with nothing under it.
 * derive also fills ctaBand.cta.text ('Get in touch') unconditionally, which is
 * why the band does in practice render on a stock build — but that is a real,
 * working action pointed at a real id on this page, not a decoration, and an
 * owner who clears it and supplies no phone, no sub and no photo drops the
 * whole <section> from the document rather than being left with a lone line of
 * type on an espresso slab.
 *
 * ── THE EMPTY HALF ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The design is two columns because the designer filled both. Either half can
 * be empty on a real site, so each cell is guarded independently and the grid
 * drops to a single 1fr track whenever only one of them exists. Without that,
 * an owner with no photograph would get their closing ask squeezed into the
 * left 52% of the band with a bare espresso rectangle beside it — a layout the
 * design never draws and that reads as a broken image. In the one-column case
 * the copy cell's right padding comes back to the design's own 32px page
 * gutter, so the text is not left visibly off-centre by the 44px that only
 * existed to hold it off the photograph.
 *
 * ── THE PHONE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * THE PHONE ANCHOR CARRIES data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. Its href is
 * DERIVED from the field's own value, so a data-href-field there would write
 * "tel:+63…" back into the phone field on the first inline edit; V3 also bails
 * out of inline editing on tel:/mailto: anchors and on anything carrying
 * data-href-field. This is the family's single documented exception to "every
 * anchor gets both", and it is the same exception CtaBandBK ships.
 *
 * ITS READ CHAIN is content.location.phone || content.contact.phone, which is
 * exactly what the sidebar's Location → Phone input reads (the schema declares
 * location.phone with fallbackPaths ['contact.phone'] and writes back to the
 * primary), so the rendered number and the field this hook opens are always the
 * same value. layout.contact.phone — the display-formatted mirror the builder
 * derives — is deliberately NOT preferred: astro-builder formats
 * content.contact.phone and ignores location.phone entirely, so on a site whose
 * owner typed a number into Location it would render a DIFFERENT number from
 * the one the hook edits.
 *
 * WHY A location.* HOOK IS SAFE HERE. editorBridge resolves a Blocks toggle to
 * the ONE section holding the most hooks of that prefix and hides only that
 * node. This page's location.* census is LocationBL (tag, headline, address,
 * hours, phone…), FooterBL (phone, address) and this band (1), so "hide
 * LOCATION" resolves to the Location section and keeps resolving there after
 * the owner hides it — a live toggle only sets display:none, so Location's
 * hooks stay in the DOM and keep winning. FOR REVIEW: the edge is a TIE, on a
 * preview where visibility.locationBlock is already false AND the footer holds
 * only one location.* hook; editorBridge compares with a strict `>`, so the
 * first host scored (this band, which sits in <main> ahead of the footer) would
 * keep the win and re-toggling Location would blank the closing band. That is
 * preview-only — the published build re-renders from the visibility flags — and
 * dropping the hook would cost the number its inline edit on every site to
 * spare that one, so the hook stays.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY (R9) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The photograph is aria-hidden, with a LITERAL unconditional attribute and no
 * conditional aria-* beside it to collide with. role="img" is not emitted:
 * there is no owner-typed caption anywhere in the ctaBand group, so the only
 * name available would be a literal I invented or the business name the header
 * has already announced. R9 says hide it rather than announce it as something,
 * so it is hidden. (Contrast AboutBL, which has about.signature to name its
 * photograph with, and CtaBandBL, which has nothing.)
 * The tel: link's accessible name is the owner's own number and nothing else —
 * no "Call …" prefix I wrote. The amber action's name is the owner's own label.
 * FOCUS: the wrapper's band focus ring (espresso outline inside an amber halo)
 * applies here unchanged. Its outline is --band rather than --band-deep, one
 * shade off this ground; restating it would fight a primitive the wrapper owns
 * for a difference invisible inside a 6px amber halo, so it is left alone.
 *
 * ── CONTRAST, on this band's own ground (#1C130C, sRGB relative luminance) ──
 *   --bone headline / outlined label  16.99:1
 *   --bone-soft-2 sub-line            10.15:1
 *   --band-accent eyebrow                    8.48:1
 *   --band label on the --band-accent fill   8.42:1 (the wrapper's measured figure —
 *                                      the label sits on amber, not on the band,
 *                                      so the deeper ground does not change it)
 * Every one of those is above AA at any size. The band is DEEPER than the ground
 * the wrapper measured its band tokens against, so each ratio is better here
 * than on the How band, never worse.
 *
 * ── CLASS-NAME LAW (R4, not inferable, do not "tidy") ───────────────────────
 * The section is .kc-close, NEVER .cta-band: buildOverrideCss emits
 * `.cta-band .btn { color: <on-ink> !important }` for every state and would
 * strand both labels here. Buttons are .btn + .btn--band-accent / .btn--line (double
 * dash) for the same reason. Never .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow /
 * .btn-light / .nav-cta / .nav-right / .trust / .testi in this family, and
 * never `.hero` on a <section>.
 *
 * ── R3 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * One scoped <style> block, no is:global. The headline is bound with set:html,
 * and elements created that way cannot be reached by a scoped rule — which is
 * fine, because the <em> inside it needs no rule from this file: PageBL's own
 * `:where(.band-dark) .h2 em { color: var(--band-accent) }` is global and already
 * turns the signature italic amber on this ground.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// ── ANCHOR GUARD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Copied verbatim from the HeaderBL sibling and pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes ctaBand.cta.href = '#visit', an id
// no page in this family renders, so without this the last button on the page
// would scroll nowhere on every first build. Resolve every in-page href against
// what PageBL ACTUALLY draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this
// design carries the same block under another name, otherwise fall back to the
// target the design was built around. Off-page hrefs — tel:, mailto:, http(s)
// and anything else without a leading '#' — pass through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBL and with the copies in
// HeaderBL, HeroBL, ServicesBL, WhyBL and FooterBL. There is no shared import by
// design (a section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`);
// the copies must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'location', 'faq', 'main']);
// This design renames four derived targets: the services block IS the menu
// (rooms lands there too), '#about' is the café's own story (host and why are
// the same block here), '#work' is the photo wall, and '#visit' / '#contact'
// both mean the location block — address, map and opening hours. A booking link
// has no checkout to reach on this page, so it lands on <main>.
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'location',
  host: 'about',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  visit: 'location',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const c = (content as any) || {};
const cta = c.ctaBand || {};

// `||`, never `??`: an owner who CLEARS a box leaves '' behind, and the sidebar
// treats '' as empty too. With `??` the empty string would be a value and the
// element would render blank instead of being dropped.
const tag = String(cta.tag || '').trim();
const headline = String(cta.headline || '').trim();
const sub = String(cta.sub || '').trim();

// The hook is always ctaBand.image, the path the sidebar's image picker writes.
// The quote escape keeps a filename with an apostrophe from closing the url('')
// early and killing the whole declaration.
const bandImage = String(cta.image || '').trim();
const bandImageCss = bandImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const ctaText = String(cta.cta?.text || '').trim();
// '#location' is the fallback because that is where this design puts the
// address, the map and the hours — the thing a derived '#visit' was aiming at.
const ctaHref = onPage(cta.cta?.href, '#location');
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);

// See THE PHONE'S READ CHAIN in the doc comment.
const phone = String(c.location?.phone || c.contact?.phone || '').trim();
const telHref = phone ? `tel:${phone.replace(/[^0-9+]/g, '')}` : '';

const hasActions = Boolean(ctaText || phone);
const hasCopy = Boolean(tag || headline || sub || hasActions);
// SECTION GATE (R5) — body content only: an action, the sentence, or the photo.
// Neither the eyebrow nor the headline counts; see the doc comment.
const hasBand = Boolean(hasActions || sub || bandImage);
// One column whenever a half is missing; see THE EMPTY HALF.
const solo = !(hasCopy && bandImage);
---
{hasBand && (
<section class="kc-close band-dark">
  <div class:list={['kc-close-grid', solo && 'solo']}>
    {hasCopy && (
      <div class="kc-close-copy reveal">
        {tag && <span class="kick kc-close-kick" data-field="ctaBand.tag">{tag}</span>}
        {/* set:html so the owner's own <em> lands where THEY put it in THEIR
            sentence; the amber italic is PageBL's global band rule (R3). */}
        {headline && <h2 class="h2 kc-close-h" data-field="ctaBand.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        {sub && <p class="kc-close-sub" data-field="ctaBand.sub">{sub}</p>}
        {hasActions && (
          <div class="kc-close-acts">
            {ctaText && (
              <a
                class="btn btn--band-accent"
                href={ctaHref}
                target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
                rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
                data-field="ctaBand.cta.text"
                data-href-field="ctaBand.cta.href"
              >{ctaText}</a>
            )}
            {/* data-field ONLY — no data-href-field. See THE PHONE. */}
            {phone && (
              <a class="btn btn--line kc-close-tel" href={telHref} data-field="location.phone">{phone}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
    {/* No upload ⇒ no cell at all, rather than a tinted rectangle standing in
        for a picture nobody supplied. The ground, the clip and the positioning
        context all arrive with .plate; this file states only what the design
        changes about them (flush corners, a 440px floor, the band's own well). */}
    {bandImage && (
      <div class="plate kc-close-shot">
        <div
          class="ph kc-close-ph"
          data-image-field="ctaBand.image"
          style={`background-image:url('${bandImageCss}');`}
          aria-hidden="true"
        ></div>
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBL's token vocabulary
     (--band-deep --band-well --bone --bone-soft-2 --band-accent --bone-rgb --maxw)
     and from
     its shared primitives (.kick .h2 .plate .ph .btn .btn--band-accent
     .btn--line .band-dark .reveal). There is no literal hex in this file.

     SPECIFICITY NOTE: PageBL wraps its primitives in :where(), so a scoped rule
     here scores (0,2,0) against the wrapper's (0,1,1) — including inside the
     wrapper's OWN media queries. Anything overridden below must therefore be
     restated at every breakpoint this file touches, which is why the headline
     size and the secondary button's side padding both reappear at 520px. */

  /* The espresso ground, the amber eyebrow, the bone .h2 and both button
     treatments all arrive from the wrapper's one `band-dark` class; this file
     must not restate any of them. It overrides exactly two things at section
     level:
       1. the GROUND, from --band to --band-deep. The design draws this band a
          shade deeper than the How band and --band-deep is that exact value.
       2. the shared vertical padding, to ZERO. The design's inset lives inside
          the copy cell so the photograph can reach the band's edges.
     No margin-top — the reasoning is in the frontmatter. */
  .kc-close {
    background-color: var(--band-deep);
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* THE CONTAINER, exactly as the design draws it: capped at --maxw (the
     wrapper's token, holding the design's own 1240px, so no literal measure is
     written here), centred, and carrying NO gutter of its own — that is what
     lets the photograph run to the band's edge below the cap, which is the
     whole point of a flush half. The 32px page gutter lives on the copy cell.
     Deliberately NOT .wrap; the reasoning is in the frontmatter. */
  .kc-close-grid {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--maxw);
    margin-inline: auto;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.05fr) minmax(0, .95fr);
    align-items: stretch;
  }
  /* One half missing ⇒ one track. See THE EMPTY HALF in the frontmatter. */
  .kc-close-grid.solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* Design: padding 96px 44px 96px 32px, flex column, centred. min-width:0 so a
     long unbroken word inside it can never widen the track (R8). */
  .kc-close-copy {
    padding: 96px 44px 96px 32px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  /* The 44px only ever existed to hold the copy off the photograph. Two classes,
     so this scores a class ABOVE the plain `.kc-close-copy` padding below and
     survives into both media queries — which is why the 520 pass restates it
     rather than relying on its own shorthand to land. */
  .solo .kc-close-copy { padding-right: 32px; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .42em tracking: one
     unbroken word would otherwise be the widest thing on the page (R8). */
  .kc-close-kick {
    max-width: 100%;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: Fraunces clamp(34px,4.4vw,62px)/1.05, margin .22em 0 .32em — the
     biggest line on the page after the h1. This is one of the two headlines the
     design leaves uncapped, so the wrapper's `.h2 { max-width: 24ch }` is
     released; the copy column is the measure. Face, weight, colour and the
     amber italic <em> all still come from .h2 + .band-dark. */
  .kc-close-h {
    font-size: clamp(34px, 4.4vw, 62px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: .22em 0 .32em;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* Design: 17px / 1.75 / #CDBFAD, margin 0 0 30px, max-width 40ch. Written out
     rather than borrowing .lede or .body, which are a different colour
     (--bone-soft), size, leading and measure on a band — every value would have
     been overridden anyway. Qualified by its own class so it beats the
     wrapper's (0,1,1) bare-paragraph margin reset. */
  .kc-close-sub {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--bone-soft-2);
    margin: 0 0 30px;
    max-width: 40ch;
  }

  /* Design: flex, gap 12px, wrap, align-items center. */
  .kc-close-acts {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* Design: the outlined action is the shared band button at 30px of side
     padding rather than 32px — the ONLY metric in which it differs from
     `.band-dark .btn--line`. This is the one button declaration in the file. */
  .kc-close-tel {
    padding-left: 30px;
    padding-right: 30px;
  }

  /* THE PHOTO HALF. Design: position:relative, min-height 440px, ground
     #2A1E14, and the slot filling it. .plate brings the positioning context,
     the clip and the isolation; only the three things the design changes are
     stated — flush corners (this is a flush half, not a framed plate), the
     440px floor that keeps the band tall even under short copy, and the band's
     own well in place of the cream one.
     align-items:stretch on the grid is what makes it match the copy's height. */
  .kc-close-shot {
    min-height: 440px;
    border-radius: 0;
    background-color: var(--band-well);
  }
  /* Two classes deep on purpose: the wrapper's `:where(.plate) .ph` scores one
     element more than a single scoped class would, so `.kc-close-ph` alone
     would lose to it. The inline background-image always wins over the
     primitive's empty-state washes, so this only has to correct the ground a
     transparent or still-loading photo shows through — cream inside an espresso
     band would read as a hole. Restating cover/center costs nothing and means
     the photograph cannot letterbox if that primitive is ever narrowed. */
  .kc-close-shot .kc-close-ph {
    background-color: var(--band-well);
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* ── R8 · this section's OWN grid ───────────────────────────────────────────
     The two columns collapse at 900px, where 1.05fr of a 92vw wrap has run out
     of measure for a 62px display line. DOM order is kept (copy, then photo) so
     the reading order and the visual order stay the same — no `order` juggling.
     The photo's 440px floor exists to match a two-column copy cell; stacked and
     full-width it is a standalone band photograph, and 440px of it on a phone is
     a crop nobody framed, so the floor comes down with the layout. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .kc-close-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
